ORGO.reg({"types":{},"items":{"sub-vs-elim-s01":{"id":"sub-vs-elim-s01","type":"sub-vs-elim","kind":"written","diff":2,"src":"FA24 Exam 2, Q7A","time":8,"pts":50,"stem":"Explain why 2-bromopropane gives different amounts of substitution and elimination products when the nucleophile/base is changed from acetate ({{CH3COO-}}) to ethoxide ({{CH3CH2O-}}). No mechanisms are required but you must identify the reaction(s) by name.\n[[mol:CC(C)Br]]","model":"<p>2-Bromopropane is a <b>secondary</b> halide, so {{SN2}} and {{E2}} compete and the reagent decides.</p><p><b>Acetate</b> is a weak base (its conjugate acid has {{pKa}} 4) but a fair nucleophile. It attacks carbon: the product is mostly substitution by <b>{{SN2}}</b>.</p><p><b>Ethoxide</b> is a strong base (its conjugate acid has {{pKa}} 16). It removes a beta hydrogen faster than it can reach the hindered secondary carbon: the product is mostly propene by <b>{{E2}}</b>, with a little {{SN2}} ether.</p><p>The stronger the base, the more elimination.</p>","rubric":[{"pts":10,"h":"Says the substrate is secondary, so both substitution and elimination are possible"},{"pts":15,"h":"Acetate: weak base, acts as a nucleophile, substitution named as SN2"},{"pts":15,"h":"Ethoxide: strong base, elimination named as E2 (some SN2 also acceptable)"},{"pts":10,"h":"Ties base strength to the pKa of the conjugate acids (4 versus 16), or states clearly that a stronger base gives more elimination"}],"solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Classify the substrate","h":"Secondary: the crossroads where SN2 and E2 are both open."},{"t":"Compare the two reagents as bases","h":"Conjugate acids: acetic acid {{pKa}} 4, ethanol {{pKa}} 16. Ethoxide is a far stronger base."},{"t":"Connect base strength to the pathway","h":"A weak base that is a decent nucleophile substitutes (SN2). A strong base removes a beta hydrogen (E2)."},{"t":"Name the reactions","h":"Acetate: mainly SN2. Ethoxide: mainly E2, some SN2. Neither is SN1 or E1."}],"answer":"Acetate (weak base): SN2 substitution. Ethoxide (strong base): mostly E2 elimination.","rule":"Better base favours E2; better nucleophile favours SN2."},"role":"practice"}},"mols":{"CC(C)Br":{"w":64,"h":60,"p":[[0,"M 7.7,52.1 L 31.7,38.3",0],[1,"M 31.7,38.3 L 55.7,52.1",0],[2,"M 31.7,38.3 L 31.7,17.0",0]],"t":[[3,28.4,16.1,11,"B"],[3,36,16.1,11,"r"]],"a":[[7.7,52.1,"C",0,0,3,0],[31.7,38.3,"C",0,0,1,0],[55.7,52.1,"C",0,0,3,0],[31.7,10.6,"Br",0,3,0,0]],"b":[[0,1,1],[1,2,1],[1,3,1]]}}});
